course: dictionary
lesson: 11210
title: Wonderful: good, amazing, or said with a sigh
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Being outdoors is wonderful, and a few simple habits keep it safe.» Here wonderful means very good and enjoyable, because the sentence is about being outdoors being a nice thing.
explain: «The sea carries the canoe to Pusat Tasek, the Heart of the Sea, a great hollow where the Wonderful Tree grows.» Here wonderful means amazing, something that makes you stop and wonder. The tree is a place to be amazed by, not a nice day out.
explain: Tone of voice matters too. If someone says «Oh, wonderful» in a cross voice after something goes wrong, they mean the opposite of the words. Check what has happened: is something good going on, or has something gone badly?
fact: Wonderful means very good, very nice or enjoyable, as in «a wonderful day».
fact: Wonderful also means amazing and surprising, something that makes you wonder.
fact: «Oh, wonderful», said in a cross voice, means the opposite: something has gone badly.
fact: Wonderfully is the adverb, so «wonderfully kind» means kind in a wonderful way.
fact: To check your meaning, swap in a word such as good, amazing or annoying and see which one fits the sentence.
exercise: «Being outdoors is wonderful, and a few simple habits keep it safe.» What does wonderful mean here? | Said crossly when things go wrong | Causing wonder, like a magic show | Dangerous and frightening | *Very good and enjoyable
exercise: «The Wonderful Tree grows in a great hollow at the Heart of the Sea.» Which meaning fits best? | Very cross and grumpy | Hidden under the water | *Amazing, something that makes you wonder | Very cheap
exercise: Tom drops his ice cream and says «Oh, wonderful» in a cross voice. What does he really mean? | *Things have gone badly | Things are going very well | He has never seen an ice cream | He is about to sing
test: «The future of AI is neither all wonderful nor all terrible.» What does the writer suggest? | It is a question about the weather | It is all terrible, with no good parts | It is all good, with no worries at all | *It is not all good, and it depends on the choices people make
test: «She sang wonderfully.» Which word means nearly the same as wonderfully here? | Badly | Slowly | *Beautifully | Quietly
exam: The rain soaked the picnic, and Jo sighed, «Oh, wonderful.» What does Jo mean? | She is delighted by the rain | *She is annoyed that the picnic is ruined | She is amazed by the picnic | She has never had a picnic
